#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:532
msgid "Editing"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:535
msgid "Adding clips and effects"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:537
msgid "Use the IDs returned by import/add/list, and discover layer numbers with ``project get``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:549
msgid "Choose the project profile early when possible; populated projects also support profile changes through the existing FPS conversion and undo system. ``--audio-only`` on clip creation suppresses video, including embedded cover art in music files. To use that clip for Audio Visualization instead, enable its video and audio, and follow the effect's help for transparent overlays and layer placement."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:556
msgid "Understanding clip timing"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:558
msgid "The example keyframes assume a 30fps project and a clip trimmed at source 10s. Position is in timeline seconds; start/end are source seconds, with end exclusive. Frames start at 1. File thumbnail frames use source FPS. Timeline frames use project FPS. Clip keyframes use source coordinates at project FPS, so trimming the clip does not move its first visible keyframe to frame 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:565
msgid "Changing properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:567
msgid "``set`` merges top-level properties. Scalars turn keyframed properties into constants. A supplied nested curve replaces that curve; use ``keyframe set`` to change just one point. JSON arguments accept inline JSON, ``@file.json``, or ``-`` for stdin. Property names, types, enums, keyframes, references, and timing are validated before editing the project. Errors identify the field, expected value, received value, and a correction where possible. GUI slider ranges are advisory where the engine supports values beyond them."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:576
msgid "Adding fades"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:578
msgid "``clip fade ID --in 0.5 --out 0.5`` calculates source-trim keyframe coordinates automatically. It replaces the alpha curve; ``--property volume --value 0.25`` instead creates an audio fade with a 0.25 peak gain. Durations must fit between the first and last visible frames; fade-out reaches zero on the last frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:584
msgid "Splitting clips"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:586
msgid "``clip split ID --at 6.5`` uses timeline seconds, snaps to the nearest project frame, and keeps both halves. It retains source-coordinate curves and duplicates effects with fresh IDs on the right half, following the editor's slicing behavior. The left half retains its ID. The response reports requested/actual split times and both summaries; a named batch result supports ``{\"$ref\":\"cut.right.id\"}``. Splits reject endpoints and locked tracks. Undo restores the original clip in one transaction."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:595
msgid "Clip menu presets"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:597
msgid "The editor's clip menu presets use the same code from the command line. Each command lists its presets in its help, and each one is a single undo step:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:611
msgid "Motion presets are relative to the clip's current transform. Look presets add or replace one effect, and ``none`` removes it. Speed and repeat retime every keyframe and change the clip's length; ``clip speed --mode reset`` restores normal speed. Other menu actions are plain edits: rotate and layout with ``clip set``, crop with ``effects add Crop``. The commands also work in ``edit apply`` and ``edit bulk``."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:621
msgid "Adding and styling text"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:623
msgid "``text add`` creates a text and places it as a clip, using the same text as the editor's **Text** tool. Options you leave out use the editor's default style:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:637
msgid "The words, font, size, alignment, box width and default colors belong to the text in Project Files and are shared by every clip that uses it, just like in the editor. ``text set`` on one clip changes all of them and lists them in ``changed_clip_ids``; ``--this-clip-only`` gives that clip its own copy first. ``clip add FILE_ID`` places another clip of a text from ``text list``. Sizes are in pixels at the project resolution, and colors are ``#RRGGBB``, ``#AARRGGBB`` (with alpha), a CSS name, or ``transparent``. ``--place`` puts the box at the center, an edge or corner, or a lower third. ``--line-spacing`` is a multiple of the normal distance between lines (1 is normal), and ``--background-corner`` rounds the background and border corners by that many pixels."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:647
msgid "A text clip is an ordinary clip. Position, scale, rotation, opacity, timing, fades, motion presets and effects all use the normal ``clip`` commands. Font size, padding, box width, and letter and line spacing set the size of the text box, so they cannot be keyframed; to animate the size of text, keyframe the clip's scale. Each clip can animate its own text colors and outline and border widths with the same keyframe commands as any other property:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:659
msgid "The animated properties are ``text_color``, ``text_background``, ``text_stroke`` and ``text_border`` (colors, with ``.red``, ``.green``, ``.blue`` and ``.alpha`` from 0 to 255), plus ``text_stroke_width``, ``text_border_width`` and ``text_background_corner``. A clip's own animation wins over the shared style, and ``text get`` lists which styles a clip animates. ``text reset CLIP_ID`` returns a clip to the default style, like **Text → Reset Style**; ``--keyframes-only`` just removes the clip's own animation. To style many texts at once, select them with ``{\"text\":true}``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:672
msgid "Applying a batch of edits"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:674
msgid "``edit apply --json @edits.json --expect-revision 12`` accepts up to 256 ordered mutation commands, using dotted command names such as ``clip.set`` and ``effects.add`` (the older ``effect.add`` spelling also works)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:685
msgid "The optional ``as`` binds a result for later commands in the same batch. A ``{\"$ref\":\"shot.id\"}`` object substitutes that result's field. References only resolve earlier names; misspellings, forward references, and duplicate names fail before live updates. The response includes ordered compact results and a ``refs`` map of actual IDs. All changes in a successful batch form one undo/redo step. ``project.profile.set`` also supports populated timelines. Opening, saving, exporting, and inspection commands stay outside edit batches. Each command's help lists its JSON parameter names, including the underscore spelling of shell flags (for example, ``--fps-num`` becomes ``fps_num``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:696
msgid "Validating batches and handling errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:698
msgid "All commands in edit batches preflight against a detached project before any live updates. Invalid input leaves the live project and revision unchanged. Valid batches form one undo group. Unexpected runtime failures can still produce partial changes; errors report completed operations. A timeout does not imply cancellation, so inspect state before retrying mutations."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:705
msgid "Precise keyframe editing"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:707
msgid "``clip``, ``transition``, and ``effects`` support ``keyframe list``, ``set``, ``remove``, ``clear``, ``transform``, ``copy``, and ``paste``. Property paths can address nested curves, such as ``color.red`` on a Chroma Key effect. Editing a nested curve preserves sibling channels. Run ``keyframe list ID`` without a property to discover curve paths and point counts. Discovery and point listings include trim/position timing context; point listings also report native frame coordinates. ``get`` and ``properties`` expose the complete native object and property metadata. Errors identify invalid fields, suggest close property names, and explain coordinate corrections."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:717
msgid "Choose coordinates explicitly with ``--space native|clip|timeline`` and ``--unit frames|seconds``. All frames use project FPS and are 1-based; seconds start at zero and snap to the nearest frame. Native is the existing engine keyframe coordinate (including source trim). Clip coordinates start at the visible clip in-point; timeline coordinates start at project time zero. Attached effects use their parent clip's timing. These coordinates address animation time, not the media frames selected by a time-remapping curve."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:725
msgid "For a clip positioned at 5 seconds, trimmed at 2 seconds, in a 30 fps project, clip frame 1, timeline frame 151, and native frame 61 address the same point. Position and trim snap independently to the project frame grid. Listing an entire curve can include hidden points with nonpositive clip/timeline coordinates. Legacy ``--frame`` still means a native frame and cannot combine with ``--at``."
msgstr ""

