#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:743
msgid "Interpolation, easing, and preview"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:745
msgid "Interpolation describes the segment **into the destination point**. Linear gives a steady rate between points. Constant holds the preceding value until the destination, then jumps. Native default Bezier handles ease at both ends of each segment. Adding an intermediate point can therefore introduce an unintended slowdown; for one eased move, start with two endpoints. Normally leave handles alone and choose a named preset for a specific style."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:752
msgid "Discover the same easing presets used by the editor's menus, without a session::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:756
msgid "Apply a preset to the incoming segment by setting its destination::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:761
msgid "The easing option implies Bezier. It sets the previous point's outgoing handle and the destination's incoming handle together, preserving other handles and point values. An earlier point must exist. Do not combine it with custom handles or a different interpolation. Presets work in batches and bulk templates. The GUI preset **ease** (\"Ease (Default)\") differs from the native handles used by plain Bezier interpolation. Back presets can overshoot endpoint values."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:768
msgid "Inspect motion **before rendering**, using the native curve evaluator::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:773
msgid "Preview is read-only and works identically for effects and transitions. It returns values and signed incoming_rate / outgoing_rate in property units per second, calculated from immediately adjacent frames. These are finite differences, not analytic derivatives. At native frame 1 the incoming rate is null. Adjacent-frame rates can inspect outside the requested range. Near-zero rates around an interior point reveal a pause; differing rates reveal a speed change. Constant interpolation reveals a jump at the destination."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:781
msgid "Without a range, preview spans the curve's first and last points. Ranges are inclusive, with 17 evenly spaced samples by default (--count: 2–256). Keyframes and their immediate neighbors inside the range are also included, so a sparse sample count will not hide a keyframe stall. The maximum is 1024 samples; larger requests require a narrower range. --at samples one frame. Preview reports requested-space coordinates and actual native frames; seconds snap to project frames as they do for editing."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:789
msgid "For advanced custom handles, both X and Y are normalized to the segment: X is a fraction of its frame span; Y is a fraction of its value change. The first control comes from the previous point's handle_right, the second from the destination's handle_left. Native defaults are right=(0.5, 0), left=(0.5, 1), with flat endpoint tangents. Changing handle_type alone does not recalculate a continuous tangent through surrounding points. Existing handles are preserved when moving a point."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:797
msgid "Keyframe ranges are **inclusive**, unlike clip-selection time ranges. List emits ``points`` whose ``co.X`` values use the requested coordinate space/unit. Pass that array to ``keyframe paste --json @points.json`` with the same space/unit, choosing ``--mode merge`` (replace matching frames, retain others) or ``--mode replace`` (replace the entire curve). ``copy`` accepts a source of the same object kind, an optional ``--source-property``, and anchors the first selected source point at ``--at`` on the target. Other curves and source objects remain unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:805
msgid "``transform`` applies a positive time scale around the first selected point, or ``--pivot``, followed by a signed ``--offset`` in the chosen unit. Frame snapping that creates duplicate coordinates is rejected, including collisions with points outside the selected range. Native coordinates must remain at least 1. Native normalized Bézier handles and interpolation travel with each point; handles are not absolute frame coordinates. ``set --handles`` changes only supplied handles. Interpolation belongs to the destination point."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:813
msgid "``clear`` retains a constant base point at native frame 1: specify ``--value`` or sample the existing curve with ``--at`` (default native frame 1). Removing the last point also retains a constant base, so subsequent keyframe additions remain valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:818
msgid "Selecting and editing many clips"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:820
msgid "Use ``edit apply --dry-run`` to validate a batch without changing the project. Use ``edit bulk`` to repeat a small command template for each selected clip, with all changes grouped into one undo step."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:824
msgid "First preview a selection::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:828
msgid "Selectors support ``ids`` (unique clip ID array), ``layer``, ``start``/``end`` (timeline seconds), ``file_id``, ``effect`` (class name or alias), and ``text`` (``true`` selects text clips). Filters combine with AND. Time ranges select clips overlapping ``[start, end)``; they do not trim those clips. Results are ordered by position, layer, then ID, and include IDs, summaries, lock state, and the current revision. Unknown IDs are errors. An empty selector is rejected; use ``{\"all\":true}`` explicitly for every clip. Selections are bounded to 256 clips and are never silently truncated."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:837
msgid "For example, save this array as ``templates.json``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:846
msgid "Validate it, then apply against the revision returned by selection::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:851
msgid "Existing-clip commands inherit ``id``; ``effects.add`` inherits ``clip_id``. Omit these target parameters in templates. Effect edits must reference an earlier named result. Names are local to each selected clip, so ``look.id`` refers to a different effect for each clip. To change existing effect instances by explicit ID, continue using ``edit apply``. The selected ID set is fixed before expansion; changes to position or effects do not change the remaining targets."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:858
msgid "``clip set`` applies absolute properties; ``clip adjust`` applies ``multiply`` then ``add`` to a numeric property or every value in a curve, preserving point timing, interpolation, and normalized handles. It validates resulting values, including nonnegative positions and volume. Structural fields remain protected."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:863
msgid "Bulk operations reject empty selections, locked target tracks, and more than 256 expanded commands. Every expanded operation passes detached preflight before any live edit. Dry runs do not reserve generated IDs. Use ``--expect-revision`` to reject stale selections. Successful batches are one undo step, including after save/reopen. GUI refreshes are batched. Unexpected runtime/listener errors can still leave a partial transaction; the error reports completed operations and undo reverts that group, as with ``edit apply``."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:872
msgid "Profiles"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:874
msgid "Discover profiles and match imported video without guessing IDs::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:884
msgid "Catalog entries include stable IDs, descriptions, dimensions, rational FPS, aspect ratios, and scan settings. List/search is paginated. Source matching returns exact IDs and ranked alternatives with explicit differences. Setting from a file requires an exact match unless ``--create-custom`` is specified; it never silently chooses a nearby profile. Still images and audio-only sources cannot define a video profile. Matching uses declared source FPS."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:891
msgid "Custom settings are embedded in the saved project, including rational rates, so reopening does not require installing a separate profile file. Project profile changes use existing frame-rate conversion and one undo transaction. Undo/redo restores clip timing and keyframes, including rounding-sensitive positions. Export can independently select a profile without changing the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:897
msgid "Saved undo history follows the editor's history-limit preference. The limit is a soft action budget: all records belonging to retained transactions are kept, so saving cannot truncate a large batch into a partial undo action. Zero disables saved history. The separate 256-command batch request bound still applies."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:903
msgid "Inspecting media and timelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:905
msgid "Inspect clip timing and track layout as text or JSON, or render images to review composition and framing before exporting."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:909
msgid "Timeline summaries"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:911
msgid "Use a text view for a quick overview, or structured JSON for scripts and agents::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:917
msgid "``timeline view`` defaults to compact text with used tracks, object IDs, timeline/source intervals, stream state, gain, and gaps/overlaps. Use ``--detail summary|clips|full`` to choose detail, ``--format json`` for structured context, and ``--start/--end``, ``--layer``, and pagination to focus the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:922
msgid "``timeline overview`` supplies compact structured context without image bytes: ordered IDs, sequence per track, timeline/source intervals, visible trim ranges, frame coordinates, audio/video state, effects, and neighboring gaps or overlaps. Intervals and frame spans are end-exclusive; frame numbers are one-based. Source frames use source FPS; timeline and keyframe coordinates use project FPS. Use ``--layer`` and ``--start/--end`` to focus, and ``--limit/--offset`` to paginate (default 100, maximum 500 items). Time-remapped clips explicitly withhold inferred source mappings. Use the returned revision with ``edit apply --expect-revision``."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:932
msgid "Frame previews and contact sheets"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:934
msgid "Render a single frame with ``thumbnail`` or sample a range with ``strip``. Use ``file`` to inspect source media and ``timeline`` to inspect the composed video, including effects and layered clips::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:943
msgid "Frame images default to 320x180 pixels; contact sheets default to 160x90 per tile, plus timecode labels. Use ``--width`` and ``--height`` to choose output pixels. Responses include exact sample/frame metadata. Contact sheets help review composition across a sequence; use keyframe previews and rendered video to assess motion between those samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:950
msgid "Timeline images"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:952
msgid "Capture the editor's track layout, including its ruler, thumbnails, effects, transitions, and waveforms::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:957
msgid "Specifying ``--output`` selects image format; ``--format image`` also selects it explicitly. The capture does not change the editor's viewport or project. Responses include clip IDs and timing alongside pixel bounds."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:961
msgid "Images default to 1280 pixels wide, with height fitted to used tracks. Use ``--width``, ``--height``, or ``--all-tracks`` to adjust the layout, and ``--track-size`` or ``--track-label-width`` to adjust tracks and labels. The start/end range determines zoom; ``--pixels-per-second`` instead derives the width from the time range."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:968
msgid "Audio waveform views"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:970
msgid "Choose ``--mode waveforms`` to inspect audio envelopes on the timeline::"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:974
msgid "``--mode auto`` uses the existing cached waveform display; ``--mode thumbnails`` shows thumbnails instead. Waveforms represent source peak/RMS levels with clip volume and timing adjustments, before audio effects or mixing."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:978
msgid "Uncached waveform extraction decodes whole sources, limited to 600 seconds per source and 1200 source-seconds per request. Narrowing the visible range does not reduce that decoding work. For longer sources, generate waveforms in the editor first or use thumbnail mode. Subsequent requests can reuse cached waveforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:984
msgid "Image output and limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:986
msgid "Image paths must be new PNG files in existing directories. Images are limited to 4096 pixels per axis, 8,388,608 total pixels, and 64 contact-sheet samples. Requests beyond these limits fail before rendering."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:990
msgid "Images are written to files; JSON responses include their paths and ``image_bytes`` sizes. Inspection waits for the image to finish, and large requests can briefly occupy the editor."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:995
msgid "Handling command timeouts"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/agent_cli.rst:997
msgid "A request timeout does not mean a mutation failed. Use the operation ID in the error with ``operation get ID`` before retrying. ``operation list`` discovers recent mutations and their states, revisions, and outcomes. Completed operations retain their results when small enough, including returned object IDs. Storage is bounded to 100 operations and 4 MiB, with individual result bodies omitted above 256 KiB. These records last only for the running session. They do not restore projects after a crash or prevent a repeated command from applying the same edit again. Export jobs have their own status/list commands."
msgstr ""
